# probe-detect-cad-misses-macos-bricscad — `make probe` never found BricsCAD on macOS

STATUS: DONE in this branch. Found 2026-08-30 the first time the probes/
suite was pointed at a real macOS runner.

## Symptom

    $ make probe-bricscad
    detect-cad: BricsCAD not found (set BRICSCAD_EXE or BRICSCAD_RUNNER)
    run-probes: could not locate a runner for 'bricscad'.

on a macOS machine with BricsCAD installed and working -- the alfe-based
BricsCAD macOS jobs in the same pipeline succeeded.

## Cause

probes/scripts/detect-cad.sh looked for

    /Applications/BricsCAD.app/Contents/MacOS/bricscad
    /Applications/Bricsys/BricsCAD*.app/Contents/MacOS/bricscad

and the real installation is

    /Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/bricscad

-- VERSIONED, with a space, and not under a Bricsys/ subdirectory. Neither
glob matches.

The file's own header says "Auto-detection mirrors alfe's backend
discovery (autolisp-front-end/source/backend-*.lisp)", and alfe walks
"/Applications/BricsCAD*.app/" (backend-bricscad.lisp), which does match.
So the comment was true of the intent and false of the code.

## Why it was never noticed

`make probe` FALLS BACK: with no CAD detected it runs the clautolisp
baseline and says so. On a macOS workstation that produces a successful
run and a results file -- just not the one you asked for. The README even
documents the fallback as a feature, which it is, right up until the
thing it silently substitutes is the thing you were trying to compare
against.

And there was no CI job for this suite on BricsCAD at all (only AutoCAD),
so nothing else would have reported it.

## Fix

Add the versioned glob, first, so the newest bundle wins:

    /Applications/BricsCAD*.app/Contents/MacOS/bricscad

## Worth remembering

A detector whose failure mode is "quietly measure something else" needs
its success asserted, not just its exit status. The probe jobs record
which product they ran (probe-results/<product>/...), so a run that
substituted the baseline is visible after the fact -- but only to someone
who looks.
